    "meta.title": "Tunneler Online - the 1991 tunnel-digging tank game, in your browser",
    "skip": "Skip to content",
    "nav.look": "Look", "look.modern": "modern", "look.original": "original", "hero.cap.m": "Split screen in the modern look - the original DOS look is one switch away in Settings.", "hero.alt.m": "Split screen in the modern look: the BLUE and the GREEN tank leaving their lit bases",
    "nav.new": "New in 5.0", "nav.modes": "Game modes", "nav.download": "Download", "nav.source": "Source code", "nav.lang": "Language",
    "hero.by": "by Norko Kaniansky",
    "hero.lead": "Two tanks under the ground. Dig through the dirt, find the other tank and shoot it before your energy runs out. Geoffrey Silverton's DOS game from 1991, rebuilt from its decompiled code so it plays exactly as it did, and extended to eight tanks, teams, computer opponents and online games.",
    "menu.play": "Play in the browser", "menu.play.i": "Free, no account, nothing to install. The free server can take up to a minute to wake up.",
    "menu.dl": "Download the desktop app", "menu.dl.i": "Windows, Linux and macOS, a few MB. Split screen and skirmish work offline too.",
    "menu.new": "What's new in 5.0", "menu.new.i": "Modern graphics, a new Settings screen, skirmish for two players, password-protected games.",
    "menu.src": "Source code", "menu.src.i": "On GitHub. Public domain (Unlicense): copy it, change it, run your own server.",
    "hero.alt": "Split screen in the original DOS look: the BLUE and the GREEN tank digging out of their bases",
    "hero.cap": "Split screen in the original DOS look - one switch away in Settings.",
    "new.h": "New in 5.0",
    "new.lead": "<strong>Modern graphics</strong>, now the default (the original DOS look is one switch away in Settings &rarr; Graphics). The same game is drawn at your screen's resolution: smooth tunnel walls, grainy dirt and cracked rock, dark tunnels lit by the tanks' headlights, dust, sparks and debris, a camera that glides with your tank, and the menus in a modern font. It changes only what you see. The game plays the same, and online every player picks their own look.",
    "tag.clean": "MODERN / CLEAN", "tag.retro": "MODERN / RETRO", "tag.settings": "SETTINGS",
    "new.cap1": "Four tanks, each with its own view and headlight.",
    "new.cap2": "Chunky pixels and the DOS colors, drawn sharp.",
    "new.cap3": "Categories on the left, their settings on the right.",
    "new.alt1": "Modern graphics: four tanks' views side by side, lit tunnels in textured dirt, an explosion's sparks, energy and shield gauges below each view",
    "new.alt2": "Modern graphics in the retro style: chunky pixels and the DOS colors, two tanks leaving their bases",
    "new.alt3": "The new Settings screen: categories on the left, the graphics settings on the right",
    "new.also": "Also in 5.0",
    "new.l1": "<b>Settings</b> in two boxes, with new options: volume, stereo, distance fade, recorded sound effects, fullscreen (also <kbd>Alt</kbd>+<kbd>Enter</kbd>), screen size, color-blind tank colors, key remapping for both tanks and the size of the touch controls.",
    "new.l2": "<b>Skirmish for two players</b>: two people on one keyboard, on the same team or against each other, with or against computer tanks.",
    "new.l3": "<b>Password-protected online games</b>: set a password when you create a game. A lock marks it in the list.",
    "new.l4": "<b>Rematch</b> takes you back to the setup screen with the same settings.",
    "new.l5": "<b>Five languages</b>: English, Slovak, Czech, Ukrainian and Russian.",
    "new.since": "Since 4.0",
    "new.since.p": "<b>Line of sight</b>: you see only what your tank could see; the rest of the map stays as you last saw it. Computer tanks play by the same rule.",
    "new.log": "All changes, version by version",
    "ways.h": "Three ways to play",
    "w1.h": "Split screen", "w1.p": "Two players on one keyboard, as in 1991. <span class=\"blue\">BLUE</span> on the left steers with W A S D, <span class=\"green\">GREEN</span> on the right with the arrow keys. On the medium map with three rounds to win, this is the original game, frame for frame.",
    "w2.h": "Skirmish", "w2.p": "Up to eight tanks: you, and a friend if you like, against computer tanks at easy, medium or hard. Any teams, from everyone for themselves to four against four.",
