    "m.gold": "gold rush", "m.gold.p": "Gold nuggets lie hidden in the dirt. Bring them home to your base; a hit makes you drop one.",
    "m.inf": "infection", "m.inf.p": "One tank starts infected, and the healthy tanks it destroys come back infected. Stay healthy until the time runs out.",
    "m.surv": "survival", "m.surv.p": "All players together against waves of computer tanks, each wave bigger and tougher, up to ten.",
    "m.hill": "king of the hill", "m.hill.p": "Hold the zone in the middle of the map. The first team to hold it long enough wins.",
    "m.shrink": "shrinking world", "m.shrink.p": "The safe area shrinks every 30 seconds. Outside it, your shield drains away.",
    "m.ctf": "capture the flag", "m.ctf.p": "Take the enemy's flag and bring it to your base while your own flag is at home.",
    "m.siege": "base siege", "m.siege.p": "Bases can be shot down. The last team with a base or a tank left wins.",
    "m.dm": "deathmatch", "m.dm.p": "Destroyed tanks come back after three seconds. The first team to the kill target wins.",
    "set.p": "<b>Settings for every game:</b> three map sizes (the original, half and double), 1 to 9 rounds to win, a round time limit, friendly fire for teams, line of sight, and what you watch after your tank is destroyed.",
    "orig.h": "The 1991 game, exactly",
    "orig.p1": "TUNNELER 2.0 was written by Geoffrey Silverton in Turbo Pascal in 1991 and released into the public domain. Tunneler Online starts from its decompiled code and follows it routine by routine: the same random number generator called in the same order, the same 16-bit arithmetic, even the original's bugs.",
    "orig.p2": "An automated test runs the original program next to this version with the same keys pressed and compares every frame: the world, the tanks, the shots and the screen. Everything beyond 1991 (more tanks, teams, modes, online play, the modern look) is built on top of that.",
    "spec.orig": "Original", "spec.this": "This version", "spec.year": "Year", "spec.by": "Author", "spec.lang": "Written in",
